//! run
//! abort
//! link: ffi/oracle.c
//! stderr: ft:34:5: runtime error: invalid release
//! stdout:
//| before
// A second `del` of an allocation whose chunk went back to the system with the state of the
// chunk is the runtime error `invalid release`, and no memory fault (D10.9 rule 4). A block of
// 2048000 bytes fills one small chunk of the allocator. The second block takes a new chunk, so
// the chunk of the first block takes no later allocation. The `del` of the first block then
// gives the whole chunk back, also the part that held the state of the allocator for it. The
// second `del` reads no byte of the chunk: the table of the allocator says that the chunk holds
// no allocation.
// D10.3, D10.6, D10.9
import extern;

// C: oracle_pass, test/lang/ffi/oracle.c.
// gives: p itself, which is no new allocation; this declaration says `own` to make a second
// owner of one allocation. writes: nothing. keeps: no pointer. calls: no fort function.
// frees: nothing.
extern fn oracle_pass(u8* p) u8 mut* own;

// C call oracle_pass: &first[0], the first of the 2048000 bytes that new gave in this function
// (D9.8 rule 3); C reads no byte.
fn main() i32 {
    u8 mut@ own first = new(u8, 2048000);
    u8 mut@ own second = new(u8, 2048000);
    u8 mut* own copy = oracle_pass(&first[0]);
    first[0] = 7;
    second[0] = 9;
    del(first);
    println("before");
    // The copy holds the address of the first byte of a chunk that nothing can access.
    del(copy);
    println("after ", second[0]);
    del(second);
    return 0;
}
